WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.609
Mission: Impossible Fallout is quite possibly
the most spectacular entry in the series so

00:00:06.609 --> 00:00:07.609
far.

00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:12.370
On top of another round of insanely awesome
action sequences, this sixth instalment also

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:15.500
connects deeply to the past five movies in
the franchise.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.790
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and today I'm explaining all the connections

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:23.419
between Fallout's characters and plot and
the other Mission: Impossible movies, plus

00:00:23.419 --> 00:00:25.950
how the ending sets up a seventh film.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:31.270
I'll be talking White Widow, Ilsa Faust, Julia's
return, Henry Cavill, Solomon Lane, the new

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:33.460
MI 7 team, and easter eggs.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:36.670
Of course, Spoilers ahead if you've not seen
the movie yet.

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:41.320
The end of Mission: Impossible Fallout sees
the newly recaptured Solomon Lane handed over

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.900
to British intelligence by the CIA.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:48.940
Taking care of that transaction is the White
Widow who CIA boss Erica Sloan refers to as

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:49.940
the broker.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:54.370
White Widow is actually the daughter of Max,
the arms dealer from the very first movie,

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:58.530
and it appears that she's in charge of the
same dubious organisation Max led.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:03.260
And the "ongoing arrangement" that CIA boss
Sloan mentions as Lane is handed over is a

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.500
reference to the deal that Max was forced
to make at the end of the first Mission: Impossible

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.970
when she was caught and arrested on the train
by the CIA.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:15.500
It looks like White Widow will be a returning
player in future Mission: Impossible movies

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:19.960
which could be interesting given her organisation's
nefarious connections in the underworld.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:24.750
I also noticed that Ethan and White Widow's
relationship in Fallout mirrors Ethan's story

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.620
with Max in the first film.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:31.390
In Fallout, Ethan pretends to be Lark in order
to meet the White Widow, just like he pretended

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:34.140
to be Job to meet Max in the first movie.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.440
And Fallout even plays up some flirtation
between Ethan and White Widow, similar to

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.170
that hinted at between Max and Ethan.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:46.119
I also thought it was incredible how Vanessa
Kirby who plays White Widow sounded so similar

00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:50.659
to Vanessa Redgrave's Max, even channelling
a number of her mannerisms.

00:01:50.659 --> 00:01:55.520
Also in Fallout's final scene is a big hint
to Rebecca Ferguson's future in the franchise

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.210
when Sloan explains that the CIA's transaction
with MI6, closes their "friend's account with

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:01.549
British Intelligence".

00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:05.830
Earlier, Ilsa told Ethan she was being forced
to continue working with the British Secret

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:08.679
Service in order to prove she wasn't a rogue
agent.

00:02:08.679 --> 00:02:13.290
However, now that Ilsa's free-and-clear of
MI6, she'll be available to become a permanent

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:14.999
member of the IMF.

00:02:14.999 --> 00:02:19.879
By the end of Fallout, Ilsa has also been
confirmed as Ethan's love interest going forward,

00:02:19.879 --> 00:02:23.689
with the movie having developed the relationship
between them, showing they do actually care

00:02:23.689 --> 00:02:26.879
for each other even if Ethan does run her
over with a car!

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:31.739
There is, of course, the matter of Michelle
Monaghan's Julia who returns for a small but

00:02:31.739 --> 00:02:33.189
significant role.

00:02:33.189 --> 00:02:36.900
She and Ethan open the movie in a call-back
to their wedding scene in Mission: Impossible

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:37.900
3.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:42.010
This distorted nightmare-like sequence has
Solomon Lane officiating at the wedding and

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:47.099
he begins to taunt Ethan about his lies to
Julia and leading a double-life, blaming Ethan

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:52.159
for her abduction in M:I 3, their subsequent
separation, and how she had to go into hiding

00:02:52.159 --> 00:02:54.360
for her own safety in the fourth film.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:58.400
This scene's important because it sets up
Ethan's state of mind in Fallout where he's

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.989
troubled by visions of losing his moral compass
and harming or killing innocent people in

00:03:02.989 --> 00:03:04.659
the course of his missions.

00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:09.450
The other purpose of this scene is to set
up Julia's reintroduction later in the movie,

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:12.019
something which Tom Cruise had specifically
requested.

00:03:12.019 --> 00:03:14.870
'I asked Tom, "what's the one thing you wanna
do?"

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:17.129
and he said people are still asking me about
Julia.'

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:18.769
'You know, you can't be with Ilsa.

00:03:18.769 --> 00:03:20.199
There's still Julia.'

00:03:20.199 --> 00:03:22.099
'And he said, I wanna tie up that story.'

00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:24.450
'So I said, OK, we can do that.'

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:28.000
'But we have to do it in a way that it's not
just something we gonna cut out of the movie.'

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.010
'It has to be baked into the narrative.'

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:34.120
It's nice to see Michelle Monaghan back and
properly integrated into the story after her

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.219
tiny cameo in the fourth film.

00:03:36.219 --> 00:03:40.579
Her scene with Luther catches us up with what
she's been doing since her split from Ethan,

00:03:40.579 --> 00:03:44.919
and she has a tender reunion with her ex-husband
at the very end where they come to terms with

00:03:44.919 --> 00:03:46.419
why they have to be apart.

00:03:46.419 --> 00:03:50.959
I think Julia's story is likely wrapped up
now in the franchise, however, she could always

00:03:50.959 --> 00:03:53.109
return if the story warranted it.

00:03:53.109 --> 00:03:56.870
For example, we don't really know who her
new husband is exactly, so there's always

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:59.780
a chance he could turn out to be one of Lane's
Apostles!

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:04.620
Also, interestingly, at the end of the movie,
Ilsa whispers something into Julia's ear.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:08.791
Although Ilsa's words aren't audible, it does
show a connection between the two who are,

00:04:08.791 --> 00:04:13.559
to quote Luther, the only two women Ethan
has ever been serious about.

00:04:13.559 --> 00:04:17.750
And maybe Ethan himself knows what Ilsa said,
given what a good lip reader we saw he was

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:19.410
in MI: 3.

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:23.500
But let me know if you've got any theories
on what you think Ilsa said to Julia.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:28.380
Similar to Alec Baldwin's Hunley in Rogue
Nation, Angela Bassett's Erica Sloan progresses

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:32.631
from an adversarial view of the IMF at the
start of the movie to a positive view of what

00:04:32.631 --> 00:04:34.280
the team does by the end.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:38.480
With Hunley's death in Fallout, I think the
film heavily hinted that Sloan will become

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.760
the new Secretary for Mission: Impossible
7.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.440
Still alive at the end of Fallout is Solomon
Lane.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:48.600
He could return as it's quite possible there
are other Apostles out there who splintered

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.070
off from the Syndicate and are ready to take
up arms.

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:55.940
Indeed, the original TV series featured the
Syndicate as a recurring criminal organisation;

00:04:55.940 --> 00:05:01.270
however, I suspect the film franchise will
want to move on to a different villain.

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:04.790
The other villain whose future seems over
is Henry Cavill's Walker.

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:09.910
After all, he did fall off a cliff after a
huge hook smashed into his already burned

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:14.680
face; however, we never saw the body and crazy
things have happened to bring back seemingly

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.810
dead villains in the movies.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:20.460
Even if there's a small chance he's still
alive, I really don't think we'll see him

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:24.890
return for the next movie as it would feel
too repeaty, though maybe he could make a

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:29.780
surprise cameo down the road, perhaps as a
horribly disfigured criminal mastermind.

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:33.970
So, this latest Mission: Impossible movie
really feels like it's making a concerted

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:39.240
effort to build a more closely connected cinematic
universe with recurring villains and new characters

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:43.460
like Ilsa and Benji returning in each film
to build a bigger team.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:47.550
It also felt like there were more call-backs
and easter eggs to previous movies than usual.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:51.930
For example, when Ilsa was gagged and tied
up by Lane, it felt like a nod to when Phillip

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:55.860
Seymour Hoffman's villain bound and gagged
Julia in the third movie.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:00.110
In Fallout, Ethan pretends to be Lark with
the hope that White Widow and Lark have never

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:03.720
met, a tactic the team previously used in
Ghost Protocol.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:08.430
This time Henry Cavill's Walker mocks the
plan saying "hope isn't a strategy".

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:12.860
The scene where Luther, Ethan and Benji set
up an elaborate fake hospital room and TV

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:17.650
show so the nuclear scientist will reveal
his phone's passcode reminded me of the fake-out

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:22.080
opening scene in the first movie where the
team use a similar scam to extract crucial

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.230
details from an adversary.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:28.230
And the final scene of Ethan climbing up a
rock face after his fight with Walker to be

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:32.900
rescued by a helicopter calls back to the
beginning of Mission: Impossible 2 where Ethan's

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:36.710
rock climbing vacation is interrupted by an
IMF helicopter.

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:41.430
So, what did you think of Mission: Impossible
6 and did it get you excited for the future

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:42.430
of the franchise?

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:45.910
Also were there any other interesting connections
you spotted?

